body:
> Good point, we still need to consider units though. A mint may support sockets for bolt11 sat but not usd because of different backends

So something like this? I think CDK and nutshell actually emit WS events based on their internal lifecycle (citation needed, @thesimplekid), rather than a notification from the backend, so it should be backend agnostic. But of course that might not stay that way forever.

```json
"nuts": {
  "17": {
    "supported": [
      {
        "unit": "sat", 
        "commands": [
          "proof_state",
          "bolt11_mint_quote",
          "bolt11_melt_quote"
        ]
      },
      {
        "unit": "usd", 
        "commands": [
          "proof_state",
        ]
      },
    ]
  }
}
```

@robwoodgate 

> So when deriving the blinded secret key for a blinded pubkey P', we need to check both candidates, (p + >r) mod n and (-p + r) mod n, and select the one whose derived public key matches P' in the proof.

Why didn't this problem emerge in the tests?

@robwoodgate 
> And just to be clear, there was never an issue signing regular Schnorr pubkey locked proofs.

Yes that's clear.

Why did we choose to invent a whole new spec for minting, instead of just generalizing what we already have n-way? For example:
```bash
curl -X POST https://mint.host:3338/v1/mint/bolt11/batch -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d \
'[
  {
    "quote": "DSGLX9kevM...",
    "outputs": [
      {
        "amount": 8,
        "id": "009a1f293253e41e",
        "B_": "035015e6d7ade60ba8426cefaf1832bbd27257636e44a76b922d78e79b47cb689d"
      },
      {
        "amount": 2,
        "id": "009a1f293253e41e",
        "B_": "0288d7649652d0a83fc9c966c969fb217f15904431e61a44b14999fabc1b5d9ac6"
      }
    ]
  },
  ...
]'
```

This way we don't introduce unforeseen bugs and we don't have to amend NUT-20.
Also, we can still succeed even if some of them were not successful. A response could be:
```BatchPostMintResponse```:
```json
{
  "succeeded": [PostMintResponse],
  "failed": [PostMintFailure],
}
```

Also, in i've changed the shape of `BlindedMessage` and `BlindedSignature` to this:

```json
{
  "amount": int,
  "id": hex_str,
  "B_": hex_str,
  "metadata": {
    "342": hex_str
  }
}
```

@ye0man - A lot of things have changed since this PR was raised. Cashu-TS, for example now implements a `JSONInt` parser to handle decimal integers over the safe limit, and an `Amount` VO to normalise amount handling regardless of initial type (string, integer, bigint). 

What started as a hard blocker in the JSON safe integer limit (2 ^ 53 - 1) is now mostly resolved in the ecosystem. 

We should still discuss whether wire forms should accept dual string / decimal integer forms, but it isn't as imperative as it was back in February.
